      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Execute the rigidity experiment workflow to test the Algebraic Rigidity Hypothesis. This involves generating constraints from a low-dimensional target function (e.g., polynomial of degree 3 or sum of 3 sinusoids), scanning constraint counts $w \\in \\{10, 50, 100, 500, 1000\\}$ and network widths $H \\in \\{1, \\dots, 50\\}$, training single-hidden-layer ReLU networks with strict optimization (L-BFGS/Adam with hinge loss), and recording the minimal width $N_c(w)$ achieving validation error $\\le 10^{-3}$. The workflow will verify if $N_c(w)$ remains constant (rigid) or grows (floppy) as $w$ increases."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom ML experiment workflow)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The experiment will produce a table of $N_c(w)$ values. If the Algebraic Rigidity Hypothesis is correct, $N_c(w)$ will remain approximately constant (e.g., $N_c \\approx 3$) across all $w$. If the Optimization Hypothesis holds, $N_c(w)$ will show linear growth with $w$. The prediction is that $N_c(w)$ will be constant, confirming solution space rigidity for the low-dimensional target function."
    },
